explanation:
Start with:

$$
I_L(s)=\frac{4}{s(7s^2+2s+14)}
$$

Using partial fractions:

$$
I_L(s)=\frac{2}{7s}+\frac{-2s-\frac{4}{7}}{7s^2+2s+14}
$$

Complete the square in the quadratic denominator:

$$
7s^2+2s+14
=
7\left[
\left(s+\frac{1}{7}\right)^2+\frac{97}{49}
\right]
$$

Rewrite the numerator:

$$
-2s-\frac{4}{7}
=
-2\left(s+\frac{1}{7}\right)-\frac{2}{7}
$$

Hence,

$$
I_L(s)
=
\frac{2}{7s}
-\frac{2}{7}
\frac{s+\frac{1}{7}}
{\left(s+\frac{1}{7}\right)^2+\left(\frac{\sqrt{97}}{7}\right)^2}
-\frac{2}{49}
\frac{1}
{\left(s+\frac{1}{7}\right)^2+\left(\frac{\sqrt{97}}{7}\right)^2}
$$

Let

$$
a=\frac{1}{7},
\qquad
\omega=\frac{\sqrt{97}}{7}
$$

Use the inverse Laplace formulas:

$$
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{s+a}{(s+a)^2+\omega^2}
\right\}
=
e^{-at}\cos(\omega t)
$$

and

$$
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{\omega}{(s+a)^2+\omega^2}
\right\}
=
e^{-at}\sin(\omega t)
$$

Therefore,

$$
\boxed{
i_L(t)
=
\frac{2}{7}
-\frac{2}{7}e^{-t/7}
\cos\left(\frac{\sqrt{97}}{7}t\right)
-\frac{2}{7\sqrt{97}}e^{-t/7}
\sin\left(\frac{\sqrt{97}}{7}t\right)
}
$$
